---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Run your own WAG — WAGademy" description="Everything you need to start a Working Accountability Group: the run sheet, the ground rules, and what to do in the first ten minutes.">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">For facilitators</p>
      <h1>Run your own</h1>
      <p>A room, a timer, and three other people. That's the whole set-up cost.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="prose">
      <h2>The shape of a session</h2>
      <p>
        Three and a half hours: thirty minutes of goal-setting — with a themed discussion in front
        of it if you want one — then three pomodoro work blocks with real breaks in between. The
        discussion is the draw; the pomodoros are the point.
      </p>
      <p>
        The topic is optional and always was. Plenty of good sessions are just the loop: turn up,
        commit, work, report. Run it bare for a few weeks and add a topic when you have one worth
        thirty minutes.
      </p>

      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Time</th><th>Segment</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>0:00</td><td>Welcome, and the day's <a href="/topics/">topic</a> if there is one (15 min)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>0:15</td><td>Goal setting — everyone posts three objectives on <a href="/board/">the board</a></td></tr>
            <tr><td>0:30</td><td>Pomodoro 1 — 50 min deep work</td></tr>
            <tr><td>1:20</td><td>Break — coffee, snacks, check-in</td></tr>
            <tr><td>1:30</td><td>Pomodoro 2 — 50 min</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2:20</td><td>Break</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2:30</td><td>Pomodoro 3 — 50 min</td></tr>
            <tr><td>3:20</td><td>Report back — wins, blockers, next intention</td></tr>
            <tr><td>3:30</td><td>Done</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        The <a href="/timer/">session timer</a> runs this plan, and a classic 25-on/5-off version
        for shorter sittings.
      </p>

      <h2>Ground rules</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Phones away during pomodoros.</strong> Breaks are for talking; pomodoros are not.</li>
        <li><strong>Goals must be specific.</strong> "Draft 300 words of methods," not "work on thesis."
          <a href="/board/">Verb + deliverable + size</a> — if a stranger couldn't say whether it got
          done, it isn't a goal yet. This is the one rule worth being fussy about.</li>
        <li><strong>No judgment on missed goals.</strong> A missed goal is data about how long things take,
          which is the single most useful thing a research student can learn.</li>
        <li><strong>Drop-in is fine.</strong> One pomodoro counts. So does turning up for the break.
          Attendance rules kill these groups faster than anything else.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What actually makes it work</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Food.</strong> Coffee and something to eat at the breaks. It is not a frivolous line item —
          it is what makes people come back and what makes the breaks social rather than furtive.</li>
        <li><strong>A visible board.</strong> Three columns — <em>To do</em>, <em>Ongoing</em>, <em>Done</em> —
          with one sticky per objective and a colour per person. We used
          <a href="https://miro.com/">Miro</a>; a whiteboard and real sticky notes work just as well,
          and <a href="/board/">the board here</a> works on a shared screen.
          Moving a sticky to Done is a small real reward at the exact moment the work finishes.</li>
        <li><strong>A facilitator who also works.</strong> Post your own goals, report back honestly,
          admit when you missed one. It sets the tone faster than any rule.</li>
        <li><strong>The same room, on a schedule.</strong> Alternate morning and afternoon slots if you can —
          it doubles the number of people whose calendars fit.</li>
        <li><strong>A themed discussion.</strong> Genuinely optional, but it's the thing that gets people
          through the door the first time. The <a href="/topics/">topics here</a> are ready to run,
          slides included — or invite someone to talk for fifteen minutes about what they're stuck on.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Your first ten minutes</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Ask the room: <em>"What's your biggest barrier to protected research time?"</em>
          Do not open with slides. The answers are the session.</li>
        <li>Explain the loop: commit, work, report. Thirty seconds.</li>
        <li>Everyone writes three objectives and posts them — <a href="/board/">verb + deliverable + size</a>.</li>
        <li>Everyone says one out loud to the person next to them.</li>
        <li>Start the timer. On time, even if people are still arriving.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Take the slides</h2>
      <p>
        There's an <a href="/slides/WAGademy-session1-intro.pptx" download>intro deck</a> for the
        first meeting — what a WAG is, the loop, and how to write a goal — and every
        <a href="/topics/">topic</a> has its own deck attached at the bottom of the page.
        Change the names on them and run them.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>The material is yours to take.</strong> Every session page has its slides attached, and
          all of it is free to reuse and adapt under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>.
          It's the version that survived contact with a real room at UQ — so change whatever
          doesn't fit yours. If you run it somewhere else, I'd like to hear how it went:
          <a href="mailto:m.massontrottier@uq.edu.au">m.massontrottier@uq.edu.au</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
